  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 434 AM EDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nThe frontal boundary remains projected to pass through the area by \nSunday night while also degrading in the process. This boundary will \neventually stall over the Florida Straits while diffusing completely \nearly this coming week. With moisture levels dropping closer to \naverage to below average in some areas, rain chances will lower \nsignificantly to below 20% for most of interior and SW Florida as \ndrier air arrives in the wake of the front. Strong surface high \npressure will re-develop as the low off the coast of the Carolinas \nbegins to advect offshore and shift more towards the Mid-Atlantic \non, which will create a stout pressure gradient that could last into \nthe mid-week time frame. Breezy winds are expected out of the NE \nwith wind gusts in the 20-30 mph range possible through Tuesday \nnight and potentially a portion of Wednesday. By Wednesday \nafternoon, the pressure gradient should be weakened enough for much \nless breezy conditions. Even with the breezy and gusty winds early \nthis week, they should stay below Wind Advisory levels.\n\nThe other important aspect of the long term forecast period relates \nto any rain potential through the end of the week. While drier air \nfilters in with the passing of the frontal boundary, strong \nnortheasterly winds over the local waters will be present in \ncombination with slower wind speeds over land, which can result in \nisolated to scattered frictional convergence showers forming along \nthe coast. This process is likely to be the main driver in any rain \nshower or storm development through the end of the week, but is not \nexpected to produce impactful rainfall or thunderstorms. Instead, \nmost of the activity should be of the meager variety. We will \ncontinue to monitor trends in case a more impactful develops for \nheavy rainfall. High temperatures will fall to the low to mid 80s \neach day in the wake of the front. Overnight lows will range from \nthe mid to upper 60s near the lake region to the low to mid 70s \nelsewhere on Monday night, then fall into the 60s across the entire \nregion Tuesday night through the end of the week.",
